WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.020
Stack and heap safety also depends on resource limits.

2
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:13.400
A design can use valid lifetimes and still fail if it assumes unlimited stack depth, unlimited local storage, or unlimited heap capacity.

3
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:22.220
Defensive C++ treats memory as a budgeted resource and makes externally influenced sizes visible before allocation or recursion occurs.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.060
Large local objects deserve attention.

5
00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:36.280
Placing a very large array or object graph on the stack can exhaust stack space, especially in threads with smaller stack budgets or code that nests deeply.

6
00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:45.580
Prefer containers or heap-backed storage when size is large, variable, or influenced by input, but keep the same ownership and bounds discipline.

7
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:51.200
Moving data to the heap is not a safety fix by itself; it changes the resource being consumed.

8
00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.360
Unbounded recursion is another stack risk.

9
00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:59.020
Recursive algorithms may be elegant, but the maximum depth should be understood.

10
00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:06.960
For input-driven structures, consider iterative approaches, explicit depth limits, or validation that prevents unreasonable nesting.

11
00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:12.340
The goal is predictable behavior under stress, not just correctness for small examples.

12
00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:15.260
Heap pressure also needs policy.

13
00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.240
Validate sizes before allocation.

14
00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:25.780
Consider maximum request sizes, aggregate memory budgets, and what the program should do if allocation fails or is rejected.

15
00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:31.400
Avoid designs where one input or request can consume unreasonable memory.

16
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:39.580
Robust systems make resource assumptions explicit so reviewers can evaluate reliability and security impact before deployment.

17
00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:43.860
Resource limits are also operational contracts.

18
00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:56.680
A library, service, or tool should make clear whether it rejects oversized work, streams it in chunks, uses bounded queues, or reports an allocation failure according to project policy.

19
00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:59.500
This is defensive engineering, not pessimism.

20
00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:04.800
Clear limits keep failure behavior predictable and help prevent one workload from degrading the whole system.
